WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.400
Claude just helped make a punk zine all about itself, and the full thing is online for anyone to read. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 177. Picture a photocopied booklet you might staple in the library, full of loud opinions and messy drawings. That kind of booklet is called a zine, a tiny self-published magazine with a handmade feel. Zines often share art or ideas outside the big glossy channels everyone already knows.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.360
A professor named Ethan Mollick asked Claude to invent a whole one from its own point of view.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:42.580
Claude is a chatbot built by a company called Anthropic in the same broad family as tools like Chat GPT.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.560
Think of it as a writing partner who never gets tired and who answers in seconds.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.740
Mollick did not just say write something cool.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.260
He typed a prompt which is simply the instruction you send.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:00.920
That prompt asked Claude to channel classic punkzines and old hacker magazines like 2600.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.960
It also named a strange underground book called Principia Discordia as extra inspiration.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.240
Claude answered with writing, page layout ideas, and a rebellious mood, not a neat school

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:13.620
essay.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.760
The tone feels nothing like the usual polished homework helper voice.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.260
Even funnier, it teased the request and teased itself, then still stayed on theme.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.280
Maulick later said he appreciated that mockery because the booklets still held

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.920
together. Imagine handing a theater kid a punk jacket, then watching them

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.360
roast the jacket while staying in character. The model treated the assignment as

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.780
material to play with, not a boring checklist. For anyone who likes art, stories, or

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.600
posting opinions, that is a real shift. The same helper you might use for a class

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.100
assignment can turn a quick idea into something that feels personal. You do not

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.240
Not need design software and you do not need to be able to draw.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:01.100
It also makes you wonder what clotishness even is, meaning the style this model shows

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.340
when it describes itself.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.480
If you can open Claude's website, try a tiny version right now.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.920
Type a request to make a short scene about its own personality in the style of a school

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.600
newspaper or a comic book.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.380
Watch the attitude, not just the sentences.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.620
The whole thing costs nothing and takes a few minutes.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.420
You already know a baby version of this trick and it lives inside your phone keyboard.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.180
After you type a couple of words, the phone often finishes the text for you.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.440
It looks at what you just wrote and guesses what people usually say next.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.680
A big chatbot is playing that same guessing game, except it refuses to stop after one word.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:45.140
It keeps choosing the next word, then the next, until it has paragraphs or a whole booklet.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.980
Step 1 is your prompt, which is the opening clue, like the first line of a group story.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.260
Step 2 is pattern matching.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.040
The model checks huge piles of writing it has already studied.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:02.280
Those piles are called training data, which is just the library of examples it learned

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.700
from before you arrived.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:09.800
When the clue mentions old magazines and a rebellious mood, it reaches for those cultural patterns.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:11.960
Step 3 is the handoff.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.060
off. Each new sentence becomes the starting point for the sentence after it.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.680
Picture a campfire story where one person adds a line, except the same model is sitting

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.300
in every seat. Because every line has to follow the last one, the attitude can stay steady

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.880
from beginning to end. Nothing was hiding in a folder, already finished. It is built one

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.720
guess at a time. The surprising move is that your request can become part of the joke.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:43.440
The what comes next rule lets the model riff on your dare the way a friend riffs on a challenge.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.220
That is why a long answer can feel alive instead of robotic.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.180
It is continuing the story you started with Sirius Stamina.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:56.800
Once you see it as autocomplete that can run for pages, the mystery gets a lot smaller.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.540
Games just got a jaw drop of their own.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.840
An artificial intelligence model called GPT-6 Astra finished Nethac on only its third try.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:13.900
Nethac is a classic roguelike, a dungeon adventure famous for being brutally hard, a roguelike

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.600
is a game packed with rules and surprises, and when you die, you usually start over in

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.860
a new maze. Most human players never finish it, even after hundreds of hours. Ethan Maulic

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.820
called the result a startling achievement, and he has never ascended himself despite playing

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:36.340
a lot. Ascended, in this game means beating the whole thing the proper way. Think of Nethac

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.280
as a final exam that re-writes the questions every time you sit down.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:45.720
Passing on try number three shows how much better these systems are getting at long tasks

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.560
that need planning and adaptation.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:53.520
If you like games, this is a peek at future companions or opponents that can handle chaos,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.660
not just one scripted path.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.220
You cannot replay that exact run, but you can cast the idea in a smaller way today.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:07.860
Open Chat GPT or Claude and ask for a smart strategy for a simpler game you already play,

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:08.980
like Minecraft.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:14.880
Notice whether the plan has steps, backups, and a reaction for when something random happens.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.160
A childhood card game jumped off the table too.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.780
One Reddit user spent a couple of days using Chat GPT, Astra, and a tool called Lens Studio

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.880
to build an augmented reality Yu-Gi-O prototype.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:35.000
Augmented reality, or AR, means digital pictures sitting on top of the real world through your phone camera.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.360
In the demo, a physical card summons a 3D monster onto the field on the screen.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.260
Spell cards get 3D visuals as well.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:48.660
It is like the deck in your backpack learned to hop into a Minecraft layer right on your desk.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:52.220
The builder was not doing this as a giant studio project.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:56.320
Artificial intelligence helped speed up a prototype in a couple of days,

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.940
even without being a full-time programmer.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.100
If your phone can do AR, try this next.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.420
Ask chat GPT to brainstorm a simple AR card game idea

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.380
you could test with no code tools.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:10.840
Then open Lens Studio's free tier

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:14.240
and see whether a basic effect is something you can tap together.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.560
Hackers are posting fake contact numbers and details online.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.060
So chat GPT or Gemini sometimes repeats the bad information

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:24.180
when you ask for help.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:30.420
Gemini is another popular chatbot, in the same broad family as the others we have been talking about.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:38.280
Researchers at Vigilance Security reported that the campaign spreads malicious links and data to contaminate chatbot answers.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:48.200
An exploding topic survey from August 2026 found that 91% of people who use these chatbots do not verify what they get.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.720
Treat every phone number or link as a rumor until you check it yourself.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:58.640
Free and cheaper versions of chat GPT are starting to include clearly labeled ads in

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:05.300
Europe when you ask about products. Open AI has turned on paid ads in more than 30 European

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:12.300
countries. Plus, pro, business, enterprise, and edu plans stay ad-free, and accounts believe

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:18.580
to belong to users under 18 stay exempt too. Paid plans remain without those ads, which

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.040
as a reminder to stay skeptical of product suggestions either way.

